// Organizations serviced by this installation. `self` is the cluster operator's
// own organization; the identity namespace's paying account is a different model.
// Account membership and operator-granted scoped roles authorize organization
// work. An account record alone grants nothing: a user must belong to an active
// group for it, and hold the applicable app/action permission.

use identity.concepts.{ user }

/// An organization: the cluster operator's own company or one of its clients.
/// The engine admits authorized organization members and cluster operators.
/// Creating/archive operations remain cluster-operator decisions; scoped client
/// administration never becomes cluster administration.
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", rankVisible, unowned="admin", clusterOwner)
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="domain", tertiary="primaryContactEmail", status="status")
// The Materializer's mark (epic memql#4977). A client is what most of the
// work this instance does is FOR, so a composition addressed to one is the
// commonest shape there is: a quarterly summary, a statement of work.
// `fields` excludes every contact detail but the primary one -- a compose
// prompt handed a full contact card spends its context on an address it
// will not write.
@composable(as="client", fields="name,domain,primaryContactEmail,status", list="clientAccountsAll")
concept account {
  ownerUserId  string   @description("The real principal owning this organization, stamped from the creator. The synthetic self seed starts unowned; successful ownership setup persists the verified claiming user. Group membership grants authorized organization access separately from this attribution.")
  name         string!  @description("The client's name, as a person would say it. REQUIRED, and it is the one thing the first-run card insists on (D7) -- an account whose name is a placeholder is a row nobody can pick out of a list. The seeded `self` row gets a starter value rather than a real one precisely so the card has something to ask for; see dsl/accounts/seeds.memql for why that starter is not a lie the app has to detect.")
  domain       string   @description("The client's OWN domain (acme.com), not a hostname this cluster serves. Free text, unvalidated, and deliberately so: it is a fact about the client, recorded because somebody knows it, and refusing a typo would cost more than it saves. Epic C (custom domains) consumes exactly this field plus v1:platform:site.accountId as its SUGGESTION source when an operator adds a custom domain to a site (D9) -- a suggestion, never a claim: nothing here verifies ownership of anything, and Epic C's DNS check is what does.")
  primaryContactName   string  @description("Who to talk to at the client. A FIELD ON THE ROW rather than a contact concept (D4): a contact concept is future work that nothing in this epic needs, and a v1 that ships two fields is one a later concept can migrate from, while a v1 that ships a half-designed concept is one it has to live with.")
  primaryContactEmail  string  @description("How to reach primaryContactName. Recorded, never sent to -- no path in this tree mails an account contact. When guest access for a client is eventually wanted it rides an invitation carrying this account's id, and the invitation carries its own inviteeEmail; the two are deliberately not the same field.")
  notes        string   @description("Free text about the client, in the operator's own words. The place the things this schema deliberately does not model go -- the billing contact, the renewal date, the name of the person who actually answers.")
  status       enum("active", "archived")!  @description("Two rungs, and the missing third is the decision (D8). The deployables ladder is draft / live / disabled because a site is a SERVABLE thing and `disabled` is the state that answers 503 rather than 404. An account serves nothing, so there is no request for a middle rung to change the answer to -- it is a record that is either current or filed away. `archived` removes the row from the default list and marks it under the Archived filter; nothing else in the cluster consults it, and in particular a tie pointing at an archived account keeps resolving, because unfiling a client must never break the provenance of what was done for them. NOTE: @default is never applied on insert (memql#2960) -- createAccount fills this with ??.")
  domainToken  string   @description("The ownership token published as TXT at _memql-verify.<domain> (epic memql#5165, section F). Minted by the reconciler the first time it sees a domain with no token, and PLAINTEXT for the reason v1:platform:customDomain.token is: the whole point is that a person copies it into a registrar, and a value nobody can read proves nothing to anybody. Cleared when `domain` changes -- proof of one name is not proof of another.")
  domainStatus enum("unverified", "verifying", "verified")!  @description("How far the ownership walk has got. `unverified`: no check has run. `verifying`: a check ran and is failing -- read domainFailureReason. `verified`: the TXT record carried the token at least once, and this is the ONLY value at which joinOnDomain is legal (D9) and a MemQL name may be reserved (D10). Never cleared except by a change of domain.")
  domainFailureReason  string  @description("Why the last check failed, typed: `dns_token_missing` today. NOT an enum, for v1:platform:customDomain.failureReason's reason -- a resolver reports things a closed set written today does not anticipate, and a value the enum refuses is a row that will not write at all.")
  domainFailureDetail  string  @description("What the lookup actually saw, verbatim. The half of a DNS failure a person can act on: `dns_token_missing` says the record was not there, and this says whether the zone answered with nothing, with somebody else's token, or not at all.")
  domainLastCheckedAt  datetime  @description("When the walk last looked. Written on EVERY pass, which is why it must stay out of every arrival fingerprint -- a field that changes every two minutes makes any digest built over the row change every two minutes too.")
  domainVerifiedAt     datetime  @description("When ownership was first proven. Never cleared while the domain stands: the walk stops at a verified account, so a later DNS outage does not un-prove what was proven.")
  joinOnDomain bool     @description("Whether a person arriving with a VERIFIED email address on this domain joins this account's group (D9). Legal only at domainStatus == \"verified\" -- a Go pre-insert validation refuses `domain_not_verified` rather than accepting a flag that would place strangers into a client's group on an unproven name. Never retroactive: it applies at arrival and to nobody already here.")
  memqlDomain  string   @description("The name this account is RESERVED under on this cluster (D10) -- `memql.<domain>` by default, filled by updateClientAccount when empty and the domain is set, and editable. Recorded now and SERVED by nothing yet: the per-account front door is a later sub-project. Refused under this cluster's own domain or equal to a front-door host, the custom-domain guardrails, because either would be a certificate request for a host the platform already answers on.")
  memqlReservedAt      datetime  @description("When the reservation was recorded -- stamped by the reconciler at the moment domainStatus becomes verified AND memqlDomain passes the guardrails. Absent means the name is not held: either ownership is unproven or the name was refused.")
  memqlReservationReason string  @description("WHY the name is not held, when it is not -- or empty when it is held, or when nothing has looked yet (epic memql#5168). Written by the front-door sweep, never by a caller. It exists because an absent memqlReservedAt meant two different things and the Accounts rail inferred which by reading the ownership stop beside it: `ownership_unproven` is a domain that has not passed the TXT walk, while `domain_under_cluster_domain`, `domain_is_front_door_host`, `name_collides_with_site` and `name_collides_with_custom_domain` are names this cluster refuses to serve. The first two of those four are record A's own codes, reused verbatim rather than re-spelled. NOT AN ENUM, for customDomain.failureReason's reason: a refusal the engine has never seen must still reach the row rather than being refused by its own schema. NOTE that `domain_is_front_door_host` is written by nothing on a cluster whose labels are today's -- every front-door host is a single label under the cluster's own domain, so the under-domain test fires first and that branch is unreachable for any name a person would type. The value exists because the code that would set it exists; record A asserts the overlap honestly rather than reordering the branches to make it reachable.")
  configuredAt datetime @description("When a person stated this organizations facts. Written by updateClientAccount or successful ownership setup. Its absence on the synthetic self seed drives the first-run organization card; boot reconciliation never invents a human confirmation.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
}
